B08d10 — partial wordexp transport repair, 2026-09-28
===================================================

This patch is part of the separately approved musl dependency supplement. It
does not replace the original source/archive or claim a complete wordexp fix.

The existing shell redirect was appended to printf inside eval. Syntax errors
and command substitutions happen before that redirect takes effect. The patch
redirects the CHILD's stderr descriptor before execl, leaving the parent's
descriptor untouched; WRDE_SHOWERR still exposes errors. The evaluated program
also clears positional arguments before expanding user words, so the helper's
source-string and redirection arguments no longer become $1, $2, $*, $@ or $#.
No environment-variable scratch slot is introduced. The same /bin/sh (dash)
and sealed image are retained, not upgraded to hide shell incompatibilities.

Sixteen small inputs check return values, word lists and stderr visibility:
  original: 10/48 checks fail in each of 12 compiler/opt/archive combinations;
  patched: 48/48 pass in each combination, 576/576 total checks.
GCC14/Clang19/EvoCC, O0/O2, reference/mixed base archives. Exact link maps verify
the replacement object; input/library hashes remain unchanged. 302 ladder
tests and Ruff pass. Compiler/image identities match musl-patched-numerics.out.

The unchanged full original programs were also replayed:
  24/24 still FAIL; no timeouts or skips, and every diagnostic transcript is
  identical after normalizing only the final executable pathname.
  Each transcript has 71 assertion diagnostics, down from 107 in the original
  control. This is a diagnostic count, not 36 distinct tests/bugs closed.
  Overall supplement result correctly remains FAIL (12 focused PASS, 24 FAIL).

Remaining failure families include parameter-expansion brace/quote contexts,
arithmetic-vs-command-substitution grammar and comments under WRDE_NOCMD,
ignored WRDE_UNDEF, and dollar quoting unsupported by pinned dash 0.5.12.
The NOCMD scanner cannot be made correct by simply permitting braces or ignoring
quotes: those contexts determine whether a command substitution may execute.
Likewise mapping every failing shell process to BADVAL under WRDE_UNDEF would
misclassify syntax errors. A grammar-aware repair or a shell protocol that
distinguishes these errors is still required. No such repair is claimed here.

Recipe (nonzero result is expected while original failures remain):
  python3 tools/ladder/musl_patched.py --suite wordexp \
    --evocc target/frontend/debug/evocc --output .build/floating-targeted/WORD-NEW
Add --unpatched-control for the retained original-library reproduction.

