Claim ceiling: repaired and tested stderr/argument transport only; wordexp and
full B08d remain OPEN.

Follow-up, 2026-09-28: musl-wordexp-memory.out records four additional allocation
ownership/error repairs in this same patch. The expanded patch passes 2304/2304
allocation-fault checks and repeats the uninstrumented 576/576 transport checks.
Original entries still fail 24/24 with 71 assertion diagnostics each. The hashes
above identify the historical transport-only result; the follow-up record binds
the expanded patch and newer compiler binary without rewriting that history.

B08d10 completion — grammar, WRDE_UNDEF and dollar-single-quotes, 2026-10-13
==========================================================================

The same patch file now also replaces the WRDE_NOCMD scanner, implements
WRDE_UNDEF and rewrites top-level dollar-single-quotes for the pinned shell.
It stays in the separately approved dependency supplement. The original
source, both archives, the test objects and all receipts are unchanged.
The pinned /bin/sh stays dash 0.5.12-12 in the same sealed image.

Upstream basis:
  - musl master now has the transport and vector fixes: 235f72897aa4 (stderr
    to /dev/null before sh starts), 331cf27023e2 (set -- clears positional
    parameters) and 38e044472edc (free the word when the vector cannot grow).
    The patch now follows 235f7289: /dev/null opens in the parent, after the
    pipe, so it cannot be descriptor 1. Unlike 235f7289, a failed open also
    closes both pipe descriptors.
  - WRDE_NOCMD uses the scanner of the 2026-06-30 musl proposal, patch 4/4
    (https://www.openwall.com/lists/musl/2026/06/30/1). It tracks "...",
    ${...} and $((...)) state, finds each $( and backquote, rejects a )) that
    does not close $(( and rejects shell-specific quote forms.
  - WRDE_UNDEF runs the expansion with sh -u, as in proposal patch 3/4.
  - libc-test cbc95567 (2026-07-18) expects $'x' to give x and $'$' to give $
    under WRDE_NOCMD. dash 0.5.12 has no $'...'. The library decodes each
    top-level $'...' by POSIX.1-2024 XCU 2.2.4 and passes the bytes as one
    single-quoted string. In "..." the $' is literal text. In ${...} and
    $((...)) it is WRDE_SYNTAX under WRDE_NOCMD, as the test requires.

Decisions and recorded boundaries; probe checks pin each one:
  1. An unterminated top-level $'...' is WRDE_SYNTAX in every mode. So is an
     escape that POSIX leaves unspecified: \q, \x with no digit or with more
     than two, an octal value above 255, \c@.
  2. A yielded null byte discards the rest of that body. POSIX permits this.
  3. Without WRDE_NOCMD, rewriting stops at the first construct that strict
     mode rejects, for example a command substitution. The rest keeps the
     pinned shell's meaning: "$'a' $(printf b) $'c'" gives a|b|$c, and
     "${X-$'a'}" gives $a. Before this patch both gave dash's meaning too.
  4. WRDE_UNDEF: when the sh -u run fails and the input has no command
     substitution, the same words expand again without -u, with stderr sent
     to /dev/null. Success gives WRDE_BADVAL. Failure gives WRDE_SYNTAX, so
     $((1/0)) and ${UNSET:?m} keep their non-UNDEF result. With a command
     substitution, sh -n only parses the same command, so no command runs
     twice. A parse error gives WRDE_SYNTAX, otherwise WRDE_BADVAL. Thus
     "$(printf a) $((1/0))" gives WRDE_BADVAL.
  5. A command substitution inherits set -u. An undefined variable inside it
     fails that command, not wordexp. The upstream test keeps this case
     disabled; the policy question is open there.
  6. If waitpid cannot report the sh -n status (SIGCHLD ignored), the result
     is WRDE_SYNTAX.

The focused probe grows from 16 to 42 inputs, three checks each, plus one
single-message check and two side-effect inputs with three checks each:
133 checks. The side-effect inputs prove that a classified command
substitution writes its file once, and that a syntax error writes nothing.

Evidence, GCC14/Clang19/EvoCC x O0/O2 x reference/mixed archives:
  GREEN: 12 x 133/133 = 1596/1596 probe checks; 24/24 unchanged original-entry
         replays (functional/wordexp, functional/wordexp-static) PASS.
  RED, previous patch 9466ac75 replayed with --patch: each of the 12 probes
         fails 31 of 133 checks; each original entry FAILS with 71
         diagnostics (24/24).
  RED, --unpatched-control: each probe fails 48 of 133 checks; each original
         entry FAILS with 107 diagnostics (24/24).
  wordexp-memory suite with this patch: 12 x 192/192 = 2304/2304 PASS.
  Standalone timing: an original entry takes about 0.07 s under the unchanged
  five-second supervisor; 15 repeated runs PASS at load average about 90.
Extra adversarial checks against the pinned dash, outside the suite:
  NOCMD safety: 4,000,000 random inputs (200 seeds, half with WRDE_UNDEF)
  over shell metacharacters and $'...' fragments. 411,450 were accepted and
  expanded; a marker command on PATH never ran. A control without WRDE_NOCMD
  runs it at once (`x`).
  Rewrite differential against bash 5.2: 36,852 accepted inputs with $'...'.
  The only differences are $0 (sh versus bash) and bash's ~N extension; no
  decoded byte differs.

Claim ceiling: this closes B08d10 for the patched dependency configuration.
The original musl lane still FAILS both wordexp entries. The NOCMD scanner
safety rests on the upstream design and the checks above, not on a proof.

